harply to 2015, 9%</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 2016, 10%</li>\n<li>Remains level to 2017, 10%</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 2018, 11%</li>\n<li>Remains level to 2019, 11%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For what model year is the percent of cars for sale the smallest?</p>","externalid":"41f938b0-0d18-48ac-b9ea-15cc947faaa2","templateid":"32c837a1-4204-45d7-803f-20ff0f5e9554","vaultid":"1cce5e4f-ddf6-4eb8-bc10-a927d5b83b42","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"31d3c0b1-68aa-45d5-b349-4eaa73a32963","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2012\"><mn>2012</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"202a6568-3fff-4976-8de5-86dd4c1c8a3a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2013\"><mn>2013</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"ba030fbc-c46a-4e95-afab-d9dcac070118","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2014\"><mn>2014</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"2f4cd34d-3c86-4d05-9f75-8dae7d52ea4b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"2015\"><mn>2015</mn></math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1691007959834,"updateDate":1691007959834}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959834, 1691007959834),
    ($SATQ$4a422e3e$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$11044-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.G.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Evaluating statistical claims: Observational studies and experiments $SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">To determine the mean number of children per household in a community, Tabitha surveyed 20&nbsp;families at a playground. For the 20 families surveyed, the mean number of children per household was 2.4. Which of the following statements must be true?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p>The mean number of children per household in the community is 2.4.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>A determination about the mean number of children per household in the community should not be made because the sample size is too small.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>The sampling method is flawed and may produce a biased estimate of the mean number of children per household in the community.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>The sampling method is not flawed and is likely to produce an unbiased estimate of the mean number of children per household in the community.</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is correct. In order to use a sample mean to estimate the mean for a population, the sample must be representative of the population (for example, a simple random sample). In this case, Tabitha surveyed 20 families in a playground. Families in the playground are more likely to have children than other households in the community. Therefore, the sample isn&rsquo;t representative of the population. Hence, the sampling method is flawed and may produce a biased estimate.<p>Choices A and D are incorrect because they incorrectly assume the sampling method is unbiased. Choice B is incorrect because a sample of size 20 could be large enough to make an estimate if the sample had been representative of all the families in the community.</p></p>
