    ($SATQ$4e527894$SATQ$, $SATQ$358c4098-f436-45ec-84bc-42d1c0fa62e0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$bf6642d9-3257-4c15-bbbb-f5e8d71b7f20$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Probability and conditional probability$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">There are <math alttext="20"><mn>20</mn>
</math> buttons in a bag: <math alttext="8"><mn>8</mn>
</math> white buttons, <math alttext="2"><mn>2</mn>
</math> orange buttons, and <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math> brown buttons. If one of these buttons is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a white button?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7abc9f39-07dc-4c01-9fc5-09146762e99a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"two twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"aa7d1c0e-1006-421f-9452-1cb1016c0085","content":"<p><math alttext=\"eight twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"ddf25588-23fb-4dd0-b7ed-f9f01964984a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 10 Over 20 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"61dcccb2-698f-4c63-ba30-bc9b0c789def","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 20 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["aa7d1c0e-1006-421f-9452-1cb1016c0085"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that there are <math alttext="20"><mn>20</mn>
</math> buttons in a bag and <math alttext="8"><mn>8</mn>
</math> of the buttons are white. If one button from the bag is selected at random, the probability of selecting a white button is the number of white buttons in the bag divided by the total number of buttons in the bag. Therefore, if one button from the bag is selected at random, the probability of selecting a white button is <math alttext="eight twentieths"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting an orange button from the bag.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a brown button from the bag.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a button that isn't white from the bag.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4e527894","external_id":"358c4098-f436-45ec-84bc-42d1c0fa62e0","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"bf6642d9-3257-4c15-bbbb-f5e8d71b7f20","uId":"e085dcde-bd57-4548-b543-5e4afd501273","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.E.","skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There are <math alttext=\"20\"><mn>20</mn>\n</math> buttons in a bag: <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math> white buttons, <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> orange buttons, and <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math> brown buttons. If one of these buttons is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a white button?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7abc9f39-07dc-4c01-9fc5-09146762e99a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"two twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"aa7d1c0e-1006-421f-9452-1cb1016c0085","content":"<p><math alttext=\"eight twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"ddf25588-23fb-4dd0-b7ed-f9f01964984a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 10 Over 20 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"61dcccb2-698f-4c63-ba30-bc9b0c789def","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 20 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"}],"keys":["aa7d1c0e-1006-421f-9452-1cb1016c0085"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that there are <math alttext=\"20\"><mn>20</mn>\n</math> buttons in a bag and <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math> of the buttons are white. If one button from the bag is selected at random, the probability of selecting a white button is the number of white buttons in the bag divided by the total number of buttons in the bag. Therefore, if one button from the bag is selected at random, the probability of selecting a white button is <math alttext=\"eight twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting an orange button from the bag.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a brown button from the bag.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a button that isn't white from the bag.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959835,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"4e527894","skill_cd":"Q.E.","score_band_range_cd":1,"skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","createDate":1691007959835,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":null,"external_id":"358c4098-f436-45ec-84bc-42d1c0fa62e0","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"e085dcde-bd57-4548-b543-5e4afd501273","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["aa7d1c0e-1006-421f-9452-1cb1016c0085"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that there are <math alttext=\"20\"><mn>20</mn>\n</math> buttons in a bag and <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math> of the buttons are white. If one button from the bag is selected at random, the probability of selecting a white button is the number of white buttons in the bag divided by the total number of buttons in the bag. Therefore, if one button from the bag is selected at random, the probability of selecting a white button is <math alttext=\"eight twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting an orange button from the bag.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a brown button from the bag.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a button that isn't white from the bag.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There are <math alttext=\"20\"><mn>20</mn>\n</math> buttons in a bag: <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math> white buttons, <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> orange buttons, and <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math> brown buttons. If one of these buttons is selected at random, what is the probability of selecting a white button?</p>","externalid":"358c4098-f436-45ec-84bc-42d1c0fa62e0","templateid":"57b1b0e0-705a-4c36-b1c2-33244cc920e6","vaultid":"bf6642d9-3257-4c15-bbbb-f5e8d71b7f20","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"7abc9f39-07dc-4c01-9fc5-09146762e99a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"two twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"aa7d1c0e-1006-421f-9452-1cb1016c0085","content":"<p><math alttext=\"eight twentieths\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"ddf25588-23fb-4dd0-b7ed-f9f01964984a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 10 Over 20 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"61dcccb2-698f-4c63-ba30-bc9b0c789def","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 20 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1691007959835,"updateDate":1691007959835}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959835, 1691007959835),
    ($SATQ$4ff597db$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$07831-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">The mean amount of time that the 20&nbsp;employees of a construction company have worked for the company is 6.7&nbsp;years. After one of the employees leaves the company, the mean amount of time that the remaining employees have worked for the company is reduced to 6.25&nbsp;years. How many years did the employee who left the company work for the company?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0.45</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">2.30</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">9.00</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">15.25</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. The mean amount of time that the 20 employees worked for the company is 6.7 years. This means that the total number of years all 20 employees worked for the company is (6.7)(20) = 134 years. After the employee left, the mean amount of time that the remaining 19 employees worked for the company is 6.25 years. Therefore, the total number of years all 19 employees worked for the company is (6.25)(19) = 118.75 years. It follows that the number of years that the employee who left had worked for the company is 134 &ndash; 118.75 = 15.25 years.<p>Choice A is incorrect; this is the change in the mean, which isn&rsquo;t the same as the amount of time worked by the employee who left. Choice B is incorrect and likely results from&nbsp;making the assumption that there were still 20 employees, rather than 19, at the company after the employee left and then subtracting the original mean of 6.7 from that result. Choice C is incorrect and likely&nbsp;results from making the assumption that there were still 20 employees, rather than 19, at the company after the employee left.</p></p>
